 To find the length of \( d \) in the right triangle \( ABC \), we can use the tangent function. The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side.

Given:
- \( \angle BAC = 41^\circ \)
- \( AB = 550 \) meters (adjacent side to \( \angle BAC \))
- \( BC = d \) meters (opposite side to \( \angle BAC \))

The tangent function is defined as:
\[ \tan(\theta) = \frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{adjacent}} \]

So, for \( \angle BAC \):
\[ \tan(41^\circ) = \frac{BC}{AB} \]
\[ \tan(41^\circ) = \frac{d}{550} \]

To find \( d \), we rearrange the equation:
\[ d = 550 \times \tan(41^\circ) \]

Using a calculator to find \( \tan(41^\circ) \):
\[ \tan(41^\circ) \approx 0.8693 \]

Now, calculate \( d \):
\[ d = 550 \times 0.8693 \]
\[ d \approx 478.115 \]

Rounding to the nearest meter:
\[ d \approx 478 \]

Answer: \boxed{478}
